          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/horizontal+composition"} horizontal composition
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-morphism"} 2-morphism
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/1-morphisms"} 1-morphisms
            is sometimes called
            = succeed "." do
              %em whiskering
          %p
            Whiskering from the left with an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence"} equivalence
            and from the right with an inverse equivalence is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conjugation"} conjugation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
            of equivalences on
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-morphisms"} 2-morphisms
          %h2#examples Examples
          %p
            An important use of whiskering is the usual definition of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functor"} adjoint functor
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/triangle+identities"} triangle identities
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            whiskering is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composition
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural transformation
            to produce a natural transformation.
          %p
            If we identify a functor or 1-morphism with its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+natural+transformation"} identity natural transformation
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %span.newWikiWord
                identity 2-morphism
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/identity+2-morphism"} ?
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/horizontal+composition"} horizontal composition
          %p In detail:
          %ul
            %li
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo ,
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F,G\colon C \to D
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi D
                    %mo →
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H\colon D\to E
              are functors and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi η
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi F
                    %mo →
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta\colon F \to G
              is a natural transformation whose coordinate at any object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              is
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi η
                        %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta_A
              %strong whiskering
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi η
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta
              yields the natural transformation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi η
